    "body": "**Hub reading 3 (inverse-time feed G93), 2026-11-17 00:46 PST, same setup, read by M5 from the screen recording and the Status tab:**\n\n`G93` then `G1 X4 A180 F5` then `G94`: **accepted, no error**. The move took about **12 s** (1/5 minute), the time G93 F5 asks for: X 0.091 / A 4.8, 0.674 / 31.2, 1.436 / 63.1, 2.79 / 125.5, 3.40 / 153.7, 3.96 / 178.3, then 4.000 / 180. Status: `3-feed-g93.nc = cycle time 00:00:14 run time 00:00:14 last line 10`, no alarm. (G94 test 2, same move at F20: run time 14-15 s.)\n\n**Summary of the three readings (PathPilot v2.14.4, 770MX SIM, 4th axis on):**\n1. **A is linear degrees, no rollover:** A350 -> `G1 A10` turned back 340 deg and read 10.000. A wrapped-A program must count A up (370, 380, ...) and never rely on the control to wrap or take the short way.\n2. **G94:** F is the XYZ distance only; A follows in the same time. A pure A move: F is deg/min.\n3. **G93 works:** inverse-time feed is accepted and timed correctly on a combined X+A move.\n\nWith blocker 1 (the #885 rate rule) already on main, both open questions for continuous wrapped-A on PathPilot are now answered. Suggested design for the post: write simultaneous XYZA moves in **G93** with each move's time computed so neither the XYZ feed nor the microARC 4's stated A rate is exceeded, return to G94 after the operation, and keep A cumulative. This issue can be unblocked.\n",
    "author_association": "COLLABORATOR",
